<%# locals: (group:, membership: nil) %>
<%#
  One group on the index. The whole card is the link rather than a control inside it, so the tap
  target is the thing a finger aims at.

  The tags say what the reader *is* in this group, never what they may do: an administrator carries
  none, because a capability is not a standing. `membership` arrives as a local because the index
  loads the reader's memberships once - a card that looked its own up would cost a query each.

  Two independent `if`s rather than an `if`/`elsif`, matching `layouts/_group_switcher`: owner and
  paused are separate facts, and a group with a second active owner lets the first pause and hold
  both. An `elsif` there told such a reader they own the group and never that they are paused.

  `bg-base-200` is not decoration: a bare `card` paints `base-100`, the same colour as the page
  behind it, so the card has no edge at all - `spec/system/groups_index_spec.rb` scores it exactly
  1.0 without this. `base-200` is daisyUI's own colour for a raised surface, and it is what
  `events/_next_up` already tints its hero with.
%>
<%= link_to group_path(group), id: dom_id(group), class: "card card-sm bg-base-200" do %>
  <div class="card-body gap-1">
    <h2 class="card-title text-row-title">
      <%= group.name %>

      <% if membership&.owner? %>
        <span class="badge badge-sm">owner</span>
      <% end %>

      <% if membership&.paused? %>
        <span class="badge badge-ghost badge-sm">paused</span>
      <% end %>
    </h2>

    <p class="text-meta">
      <%= pluralize group.current_members.count, "member" %><%= EventsHelper::DOT %><%= next_event_line group %>
    </p>
  </div>
<% end %>
